  <section id="dangers">
    <h2>The Dangers of Wild Swimming Rivers</h2>
    <p>
      Beyond water quality, the physical dangers of wild swimming rivers must be respected. Moving water possesses immense force, and surface calmness can hide treacherous undercurrents.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Strainers:</strong> Fallen trees or branches trap debris and swimmers while letting water flow through. Avoid them at all costs.</li>
      <li><strong>Weirs:</strong> These man-made structures create a recirculating current at the base, known as a "washing machine," which can trap even the strongest swimmers.</li>
      <li><strong>Cold Water Shock:</strong> Sudden immersion in water below 15°C can cause involuntary gasping and hyperventilation. [Source: National Water Safety Forum]</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="safety-tips">
    <h2>Essential Wild Swimming Safety Tips</h2>
    <p>
      To mitigate these risks, always adhere to strict wild swimming safety tips. Never swim alone in an unfamiliar stretch of river. Always identify a clear, accessible exit point before you enter the water—muddy banks can be incredibly difficult to climb when you are cold and fatigued.
    </p>
